---
import CodeBlock from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ContentSection from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/ContentSection.astro";
import InlineCode from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/InlineCode.astro";
import { InComponentEcho } from "../../components/demos/InComponentEcho";
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/link";

export const prerender = true;
---

<DocsPage title="In-Component Echo" description="The dead-simple starting point: create a manager, send a message, display the response. Everything in one component.">
	<ContentSection label="In-Component Echo">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
			This is the absolute minimum you need to get react-socket working. One component,
			one message type, no store, no effects for lifecycle management. Create the manager
			in a <InlineCode>useMemo</InlineCode>, store the response in
			<InlineCode>useState</InlineCode>, call
			<InlineCode>send()</InlineCode>. That's it.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			This pattern is fire-and-forget: you send a message and move on. No acknowledgment
			tracking, no subscriptions, no external state. It's the right starting point when
			you want to see the library work before layering in complexity.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Live Demo">
		<InComponentEcho client:load />
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Source">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			The entire example. Notice how
			<InlineCode>TClientMsg</InlineCode> and
			<InlineCode>TServerMsg</InlineCode> type the manager generics, so
			every <InlineCode>send()</InlineCode> call and every
			<InlineCode>onMessageReceived</InlineCode> callback is fully typed:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import { WebSocketManager } from "@luciodale/react-socket";

type TClientMsg = { action: "echo"; text: string };
type TServerMsg = { action: "echo"; text: string };

function EchoDemo() {
  const [response, setResponse] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const manager = useMemo(() => {
    const m = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
      url: "ws://localhost:3001/ws",
      serialize: (msg) => JSON.stringify(msg),
      deserialize: (raw) => JSON.parse(raw),

      onMessageReceived(msg) {
        setResponse(msg.text);
      },
    });
    m.connect();
    return m;
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => manager.send({ data: { action: "echo", text: "hello" } })}>
        Send
      </button>
      {response && <p>Server said: {response}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="EchoDemo.tsx"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			From here, you can layer in
			<a href={href("/docs/subscriptions")} class="text-accent hover:underline">subscriptions</a> for
			real-time data streams,
			<a href={href("/docs/optimistic-updates")} class="text-accent hover:underline">acknowledged sends</a> for
			delivery tracking, or move the manager out of the component into a shared module.
			See the <a href={href("/demo/fire-and-forget")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Fire and Forget</a>
			example for a slightly richer version with input handling and message history.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>
</DocsPage>
